summary:
Weil II passes from the cohomology of varieties to cohomology with coefficients in a sheaf. It introduces pure and mixed ℓ-adic (and Weil) sheaves on schemes of finite type over F_q (§1.1–1.2). Its main theorem (Théorème 1 = 3.3.1) says that if ℱ₀ is ι-mixed of weights ≤ n, then R^i f_!ℱ₀ is ι-mixed of weights ≤ n + i; in particular H^i_c(X, ℱ) has weights ≤ n + i. The proof runs through determinantal weights and Grothendieck's theorem that the radical of the geometric monodromy group is unipotent (1.3). It uses the monodromy (weight) filtration of a nilpotent endomorphism and local monodromy of pure sheaves, 1.8.4 (§§1.6–1.9), non-archimedean bounds (1.10) and specialization of monodromy (1.11). It adapts the Hadamard–de la Vallée-Poussin method (§2) to prove the initial H¹ bound for curves (3.2.3). Applications:
- the structure of mixed sheaves, with the weight filtration and geometric semisimplicity of pure sheaves (3.4);
- equidistribution of Frobenius conjugacy classes, with the function-field Sato–Tate theorem (3.5);
- the local invariant cycle theorem (3.6);
- the hard Lefschetz theorem over finite fields (4.1.1), deduced with a refined study of Lefschetz-pencil monodromy (open, or finite and a Weyl group of type ADE) and the gcd theorem (§4);
- weight gradings on the ℓ-adic homotopy type and formality of proper smooth varieties (§5);
- stability of mixed complexes under the six operations, and purity of Rf_* of pure complexes for proper f (§6).

The atlas was built for this paper. DeligneWeightsAndPurity DWP.5–DWP.9 plan it almost step by step, LefschetzPencilsAndVanishingCycles LPV.1–LPV.7 plan the local and global monodromy, and EtaleDualityAndPerverseSheaves plans the duality formalism. Of 505 items, 302 are planned, 5 are in the libraries (Tau Ceti's sl₂ representation theory and Clebsch–Gordan, finiteness of the degree-zero divisor class group, and Mathlib's nonvanishing of ζ on Re s ≥ 1), and 198 are missing. The missing items take six routes. Five are sources of existing layers: DeligneWeightsAndPurity (88), LefschetzPencilsAndVanishingCycles (60), SchemeAndStackFoundations SF.2 (9), FiniteFieldsAndCharacterSums FF.2 (2) and EtaleDualityAndPerverseSheaves EDC.4 (1). The sixth is one new roadmap, EllAdicHomotopyTypesAndWeights, for §5, which no layer plans: the Grothendieck–Miller divided-power de Rham complex, the ℓ-adic homotopy type A(X), and weight gradings and formality (38 items). It imports DG-algebra theory from the Tau Ceti DGAInfinity roadmap.
